<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uromi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uromi is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and one of the largest of the Esan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, ruled by its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onojie">Onojie</a>. Like other Esan chiefdoms it traced its ruling house to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Kingdom of Benin</a> while largely running its own affairs. After the British conquest of Benin in 1897, Uromi resisted British occupation until 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uromi.jpg" alt="Uromi" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omorodion1 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uromi_Main_Market.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Uromi, originally known as Uronmun, lies in north-eastern Esanland, in Esan North-East in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a>, and is the most populous area of Esanland. It is a conurbation of towns and villages in three groups, Okhiode, Obiruan and Obiyon. Set in the rain forest zone, it has two rainy seasons, the heavier from April to July and a weaker one in October and November, and a long dry season from December to March, when harmattan winds blow from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was settled in several waves. The first settlers, believed to have come from present-day central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, lived in autonomous villages as early as 100 CE, each governed by its eldest man. Later migrants came from other parts of Esanland, from Ifeku Island and from Igodomigodo, and the kingdom itself was formed around 1100 to 1200. Under Agba N'Ojie the people of Uromi resisted the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a> during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozolua">Oba Ozolua</a>, and they were later at the forefront of resistance to the British invasion of Esanland. The list of kings runs from Ijiesan through Agba N'Ojie and Okolo N'Ojie to the Edenojie line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The kingdom is a hereditary monarchy whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onojie">Onojie</a> rules with a body of chiefs, while councils of elders, each headed by the eldest male indigene, govern the villages and may punish offenders. The seven villages of Eguare, the Onojie's seat, crown each new king. Uromi is the seat of the Esan North-East local government council, and its economy rests on farming and trading. Its festivals include Oto-Uromi, held in July or early August on a market day fixed by the Onojie to appease the land for a good harvest, and Amukpe, held in August to usher in the new yams.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uromi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Princes and nobles from Benin became Enijie (singular Onojie), founding chiefdoms such as <b>Uromi</b>, Ekpoma, Irrua and Ubiaja.</blockquote><a href="/state/esan-chiefdoms">Esan chiefdoms</a></li></ul></section>
